# Numeric precision domain rule

Rule: `CUSTOM:DOM09` · Source version: `formsy-numeric-precision-0.1`.
Use the existing [domain check card](sop-rules-addendum-20261016/domain-check-card.md).

Enable this rule for money or any quantity whose stated contract requires
exact representation, calculation, reconciliation, or controlled rounding.
Record `applicable`, `not_applicable`, or `pending` and the actual trigger
or missing decision on the card. A project that only displays text does not
need to add money or multi-currency functionality. Payment checks in DOM08
can coexist with this rule; reuse evidence where it applies.

## Decide the precision contract before implementation

In the specification state the unit (and currency where applicable), accepted
input grammar, scale, per-value and aggregate ranges, record volume, and
output format. Decide the bounds and precision of intermediate calculations,
rounding mode and point of rounding, negative values/refunds, and any
allocation remainder and its tie-break. Define the result for invalid or
out-of-range inputs and whether rejection is atomic. Mark irrelevant
operations as such. Bring material unknowns to clarification; don't pick an
unapproved cap to make existing calculations pass.

In the plan show the representation at input, calculation, storage, API,
serialization, and display boundaries. For fixed-scale addition/subtraction,
consider parsing the original string directly into integer minor units.
Set the scale from the agreed unit and contract. Check integer range in each
system, including database columns and API consumers. Use an exact string or an
integer carried by an exact encoding where a consumer's numeric type cannot
represent the value. Avoid round trips through binary floating point. Rate,
tax, division, FX and allocation may require different intermediate precision
and a separately declared rounding contract. The final allocation, including
its remainder, should reconcile to the amount allocated under that contract.

If using Python `Decimal`, construct from source strings. Use an explicit
local context independent of the caller's, covering the worst legal
intermediates, exponent range and final quantization. A finite fixed-scale
sum can use a bound such as record count times the maximum absolute input in
minor units to bound intermediate magnitudes. No fixed precision alone proves
an unbounded input contract. Specify how `Inexact`, `Rounded` and
`InvalidOperation` are handled. For example, reject unexpected inexact
arithmetic while permitting an explicitly authorized rounding operation.
`Rounded` can indicate that only zero digits were dropped; it does not always
mean the value changed. Preserve the agreed error contract rather than
silently weakening it.

## Carry one card through the existing flow

Keep the card ID, source version and locator, actual trigger, applicability,
scope, and gaps together. Fill the following links on that card; use the real
IDs and versions from the project, not the example names.

| Existing stage | What to bind on the same card |
| --- | --- |
| Project Principles | Record the precision invariant and source if it is an authorized project principle. When reusing existing principles, record their locator or the task-specific trigger instead of rewriting them. |
| Requirements | Link the relevant FR/SC IDs in `spec.md` to the input, precision, rounding and observable error contract. Retain decisions still awaiting clarification. |
| Technical Planning | Link the corresponding object in `plan.md` to its representation at each boundary and the precision, limits, rounding, and recovery decisions. |
| Task Breakdown and Preview | Link the real `tasks.md` IDs and `analysis.md` evidence. In the existing task packet's test rows, give each test a precondition, data, independent expected result, and intended receipt. Freeze the relevant existing requirement items and program artifacts/commands in the [acceptance scope](acceptance-scope.md). |
| Self-check and Acceptance | Check the spec and plan against the actual contract. A type name or normal example alone leaves the obligation unverified. Run the original frozen [workflow checks](workflow.md) on the current candidate. Keep actual outcomes and receipts linked to those same requirements, including failures and gaps. |

Document consistency and actual execution use separate evidence. For
Workbench visibility, register the card as the current SOP or a named
attachment through `document_changes` with its real path, revision and
digest. Keep `CUSTOM:DOM09` out of compiled `condition_ids` and the legacy
PM/M denominator. Add real application checks through the existing
requirement-based acceptance scope; a domain card creates neither a Verifier
nor a program pass. Keep frozen receipts and previous scope versions as saved.

## Executable acceptance examples

The following example is a single-unit CSV with an optional minus sign, a
decimal integer or one/two fractional digits, and negative refunds. The
original request declares no digit ceiling. Let
`H = 100000000000000000000000000.00`. Literal expected results below are
independent of the implementation. Preserve the old 32-case result as history.

| Run and compare | Expected result and what it catches |
| --- | --- |
| `0.10 + 0.20` | `0.30`. This happy path also passes with the flawed Decimal sum, so it is insufficient evidence. |
| Read CSV rows in this order: `2026-01-01,A,H`, `2026-01-01,B,0.01`, `2026-01-01,A,-H` (substitute the exact `H`). Then place the two A rows first, try other permutations, and merge independently summed batches. | Every pure sum is `0.01`; A is `0.00`, B is `0.01`, and their sum equals the total. Default precision 28 can produce `0.00` in the first order while A and B still reconcile to `0.01`. |
| Run `10000.00 + 0.01 - 10000.00` with caller precision 6. Process the single large `H` with default caller precision 28. | Preserve `0.01` and `H`, respectively. Ambient-context addition can lose the cent; ambient quantization can raise `InvalidOperation` for the legal singleton. |
| Round-trip `-1.2`, `0.01` and `H` through storage/API encoding and back to canonical strings. | `-1.20`, `0.01`, and `H` preserve their values; demonstrate the actual representation, not only arithmetic. |
| If a different contract declares a magnitude/aggregate cap, test its legal limit and one minor unit past it. If the contract rounds, test ties and the agreed point of rounding with matching expected values. | For an example cap of `10.00`, accept `10.00`, reject `10.01` with the declared error and no partial result. Two exact `0.005` intermediate values give `0.02` for per-item half-up, `0.00` for per-item half-even, and `0.01` for half-up on their total. These are separate contracts, not constraints imposed on the CSV example. |

For exact sums, include representative maximum record counts and cumulative
values where the project has such limits. For business rounding or allocation,
verify the declared operation order and reconciliation; do not require order
invariance where the contract makes order significant. A completed check
names the tested candidate, input and expected value, actual observation, and
version-bound receipt. Missing or differing results do not pass.

## Source notes

Sources checked on 2026-11-17 (Asia/Shanghai). This rule and its examples are
FormSy guidance, with the original counterexample from
[issue #265](https://github.com/FormSyAI/formsy-sop/issues/265).

| Source | What was used |
| --- | --- |
| [ISO 4217:2015 public abstract](https://www.iso.org/standard/64758.html) | Edition 8 public abstract: currency codes and the relationship to minor units where present. The full standard was not consulted; this rule makes no claim that ISO mandates integer storage. |
| [OpenSSF Python Secure Coding Guide, pyscg-0001](https://best.openssf.org/Secure-Coding-Guide-for-Python/03_numbers/pyscg-0001/) | Engineering guidance on selecting exact numeric representations when precision matters. |
| [Python 3.12 `decimal` documentation](https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/decimal.html) | Language behavior: precision, cancellation, `localcontext`, rounding modes, `quantize` and signals. |
| [Stripe amount formatting](https://docs.stripe.com/currencies#specify-amounts-in-api-requests) | A provider's minor-unit request contract, with provider-specific scales, exceptions and limits. |
